    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • Ford Frostwarden, a Bronco SUV With Over 1,200 HP, the R125 and F-250 Also in Tow\nVW Explores Turkish Vehicle Production of Chinese-Developed Models\n8 photos\nPhoto: Volkswagen\nVolkswagen has a few problems in China. The biggest one is finding more customers for the vehicles it produces in China with its joint venture partners. Turkey could help with that, and the German automaker seems interested in local assembly of Chinese-developed models with Dogus Otomotiv.\nThis arrangement would offer the Wolfsburg-based group another way to use production capacity that its Chinese business no longer needs as badly due to shrinking demand in the People's Republic of China. During the first nine months of 2026, the group delivered 1.38 million vehicles in the PRC, down a whopping 30 percent from the same period last year.\nInternal combustion models took a particularly dramatic hit as domestic manufacturers and their electrified models continued to gain serious ground. Keeping factories busy is considerably hard when the market that once absorbed millions of VWs starts buying fewer of them. Worse still, Volkswagen has spent decades building its Chinese operations around local demand.\nThe proposed Turkish partnership could provide an outlet. However, as of this writing, the agreement itself remains preliminary. Dogus Otomotiv confirmed that it had signed a letter of intent with the group to explore possibilities for producing said vehicles in the Republic of Turkiye. The disclosure is devoid of any confirmed models, production targets, or investment figures.\nHearsay suggests Volkswagen and Dogus could assemble FAW-Volkswagen models, and the rumor mill further suggests that CKD production is under discussion. CKD means completely knocked-down kit, and in this context, it stands for shipping disassembled vehicles from China to Turkey.\nPhoto: Volkswagen Rather than manufacturing every component locally, Volkswagen would use Chinese plants for component production and Turkey for vehicle assembly from these kits. The arrangement would provide additional work for its ailing Chinese plants while giving Dogus a more important role in the Turkish automotive industry.\nWhy Turkey, though? Per Automotive News, Turkey is a large new passenger vehicle market. Even more importantly, electric vehicles account for just under 20 percent of total registrations. That makes Turkey a useful market for a company that is trying to find customers for Chinese-developed gasoline models.\nThe potential connection to FAW-Volkswagen also needs highlighting because this joint venture has already started testing the export route. Its gasoline-powered models have reached markets in Central Asia and the Middle East, and the joint venture is also exporting vehicles to Madagascar. As such, the proposed Turkish operation would complement those efforts.\nVolkswagen , meanwhile, is certainly thinking beyond Turkey. The group has increasingly moved away from its former Chinese approach. The group currently wants to use Chinese development and manufacturing capabilities for models that can find buyers elsewhere, as proven by the FAW-Volkswagen models exported to Madagascar, the Middle East, and Central Asia.\nOh, and by the way, Turkey isn't new territory for Volkswagen's manufacturing ambitions. The company abandoned plans for a factory there in 2020, citing the pandemic and economic uncertainties. That proposal, however, would have involved a dedicated production investment. Dogus, by comparison, would allow the German automaker to establish a production arrangement without committing to building an entirely new facility.\nVia // Automotive News\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nVolkswagen Dogus Turkey China production\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Royal Enfield Unloads Four New Motorcycles, Custom Son of a Gun Takes Center Stage",
